  },
  "question": {
    "text": "Two massless springs with spring constants <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></math>, carry <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> masses at their free ends. These two masses oscillate vertically such that their maximum velocities are equal. Then, the ratio of their respective amplitudes will be :"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>2</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>2</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>3</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Maximum velocity is given by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mi>A</mi></math></p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0061_e60ff92a90c2.png\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 179px;\" /></p><p>Here,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></msqrt><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></msqrt><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p><p>&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p><p>&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p><p>Thus, ratio of amplitude is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>2</mn></math>.</p>"
  }
